  'This Year 4 elective-area course prepares kinesiology students to design, deliver, evaluate, and advocate for inclusive physical activity across schools, recreation, sport, fitness, health-promotion, and community settings for people with disabilities across the lifespan.',
  $apajson${"slug": "adapted-physical-activity", "title": "Adapted Physical Activity", "description": "This Year 4 elective-area course prepares kinesiology students to design, deliver, evaluate, and advocate for inclusive physical activity across schools, recreation, sport, fitness, health-promotion, and community settings for people with disabilities across the lifespan.", "topics": [{"id": "5c23ca51-2895-44a4-bf07-6a05b8870480", "type": "topic", "title": "Disability, health equity, and physical activity participation", "description": "Adapted physical activity is about participation, dignity, and health, not simply modified exercises. This section frames disability-inclusive movement as a public health, education, sport, and human rights issue.", "children": [{"id": "9d661540-9f47-486f-8827-e300576ede76", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Participation, health, and belonging", "description": "Students learn why access to movement matters for physical health, social connection, self-efficacy, and lifelong wellbeing.", "children": [{"id": "783f1f63-0064-4722-bddc-235e3c5ad9ac", "type": "concept", "title": "Physical activity disparities and disability-related barriers"}, {"id": "61789233-0eaa-49cb-9d79-0153f588b73c", "type": "concept", "title": "Health promotion, chronic disease prevention, and quality of life"}, {"id": "03326ddb-7c43-4105-acaa-ed20d42d20cd", "type": "concept", "title": "Belonging, autonomy, competence, and self-determination"}, {"id": "66d21bef-b197-478b-a485-ebd0cc70fbbb", "type": "concept", "title": "School, recreation, sport, fitness, and community participation pathways"}]}, {"id": "e3060499-465f-4f3c-b6f0-bdffb15460ce", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Equity barriers and systems thinking", "description": "The gist is to see barriers as design problems in environments and systems, not deficits located only in the person.", "children": [{"id": "b2b93272-ecdd-46e3-bd6e-ae0a603eb5c6", "type": "concept", "title": "Transportation, cost, staffing, policy, and facility barriers"}, {"id": "57b5b4b2-80d9-46de-8f26-2cfa717972e3", "type": "concept", "title": "Ableism, stigma, low expectations, and exclusionary norms"}, {"id": "f9abde73-26db-42c4-b343-15ca2b6eaee3", "type": "concept", "title": "Intersectionality across disability, race, gender, class, and language"}, {"id": "85c3a0f5-14f4-4b0b-bb1b-5126d53b390c", "type": "concept", "title": "Program-level strategies for equitable access and retention"}]}]}, {"id": "a0bdab64-6f68-4555-81a0-fc60bbb4cfd4", "type": "topic", "title": "Legal, ethical, and professional foundations: ADA, IDEA, Section 504, and inclusion", "description": "Inclusive practice has legal and ethical foundations. This section helps students understand the rules and professional duties that shape access, accommodations, and participation.", "children": [{"id": "f41f536f-81c2-4834-ad33-236f49f71274", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Core legal frameworks", "description": "Students learn the main legal concepts that govern disability access in schools, public programs, and community settings.", "children": [{"id": "545523da-7943-4c77-a5b8-e4f69d9e4990", "type": "concept", "title": "Americans with Disabilities Act and reasonable accommodation"}, {"id": "933c1f3a-7803-4383-a789-a394ddec1020", "type": "concept", "title": "Individuals with Disabilities Education Act and adapted physical education"}, {"id": "684b5e0a-472f-4ae6-8c50-9385359b0d22", "type": "concept", "title": "Section 504, least restrictive environment, and equal access"}, {"id": "8d0b2576-e434-499a-9a2e-b68441c151f8", "type": "concept", "title": "Individualized Education Programs, service delivery, and documentation"}]}, {"id": "77caad06-767a-4965-8ec9-9de05999db7b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ethical and professional practice", "description": "The point is to make inclusion defensible, respectful, and aligned with professional standards rather than symbolic.", "children": [{"id": "3077c203-5ad1-41b7-a490-b2aa6ca5e742", "type": "concept", "title": "Informed consent, assent, privacy, and confidentiality"}, {"id": "759c0062-fa84-40f9-bb91-0a82ac0f883b", "type": "concept", "title": "Risk-benefit decisions and duty of care"}, {"id": "07ca4e5d-05be-4d17-9322-4dc91c9306c9", "type": "concept", "title": "Scope of practice, referral, and interdisciplinary collaboration"}, {"id": "4bb3102f-97c5-4f26-98b7-90d8d0e1cd7a", "type": "concept", "title": "Professional advocacy and accountability for inclusive environments"}]}]}, {"id": "116d6610-0750-4ea0-96b7-d9e54616a14e", "type": "topic", "title": "Disability models, identity, person-centered language, and cultural humility", "description": "How practitioners think and speak about disability shapes the programs they build. This section develops respectful, participant-centered, culturally humble practice.", "children": [{"id": "98e77604-9b28-4121-89bb-44cc08417abf", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Models of disability and identity", "description": "Students learn to compare medical, social, biopsychosocial, and rights-based models and apply them to program decisions.", "children": [{"id": "fc53530a-7899-4571-a083-308da6d24fe9", "type": "concept", "title": "Medical, social, and ecological perspectives"}, {"id": "7f6cfb1f-9c04-437b-a2e4-84a214cba2be", "type": "concept", "title": "Disability culture, pride, identity, and lived experience"}, {"id": "f8cd0d1f-f1f6-4904-ae9d-117c9de44e2e", "type": "concept", "title": "Strengths-based versus deficit-based planning"}, {"id": "52cf033e-ced9-499a-9f1f-8b0032d8b5d9", "type": "concept", "title": "Listening to participants, families, and disability communities"}]}, {"id": "91893493-7a3c-4729-a3d3-d8f8128572a5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Language and cultural humility", "description": "The gist is to communicate respectfully while recognizing that preferences vary by person, culture, and context.", "children": [{"id": "772df468-9bb7-4b4a-b856-f8ca2367ba50", "type": "concept", "title": "Person-first and identity-first language preferences"}, {"id": "568d1872-32b8-4b58-996a-0a14e44e9d42", "type": "concept", "title": "Avoiding inspiration narratives, pity, and low expectations"}, {"id": "ab54f506-136b-4b98-8cc4-2a187e89e0e8", "type": "concept", "title": "Culturally responsive coaching and family engagement"}, {"id": "e230b991-d0b4-4c42-8dd1-c10e14241f1c", "type": "concept", "title": "Reflective practice, bias checks, and feedback loops"}]}]}, {"id": "e9a4b2e6-e9fc-45fb-ac84-e90cf5cd6bfc", "type": "topic", "title": "Intellectual disability, developmental disability, and neurodiversity", "description": "Participants with intellectual and developmental disabilities often need changes in instruction, pacing, communication, and environment. This section focuses on practical teaching that supports autonomy and success.", "children": [{"id": "2ad2e128-8345-4059-8393-0266ad387e88", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Learning, communication, and motor planning adaptations", "description": "Students learn to break activities into accessible parts while preserving meaningful challenge and choice.", "children": [{"id": "9f64383a-a698-4453-ad02-22c90e3a2096", "type": "concept", "title": "Task analysis, chaining, prompting, fading, and visual supports"}, {"id": "d49e8f1c-7c96-4a24-a24a-48ce02d4edbc", "type": "concept", "title": "Concrete instructions, modeling, repetition, and consistent routines"}, {"id": "287ec528-b618-47b5-91df-89c9b6dfb5fe", "type": "concept", "title": "Motor planning, coordination, balance, and movement confidence"}, {"id": "8e10252a-228f-4469-994d-d949eb49a20f", "type": "concept", "title": "Choice-making, self-monitoring, and participant voice"}]}, {"id": "8a92c929-547c-4516-ad22-f5f9bd16d941", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Neurodiversity and self-regulation", "description": "The point is to support participation for autistic and neurodivergent participants without forcing conformity or ignoring sensory needs.", "children": [{"id": "e6e7a423-ba9e-4a0a-abc2-46dc149d0472", "type": "concept", "title": "Sensory profiles, predictability, transitions, and regulation supports"}, {"id": "a6ab6a58-d1a7-4f79-ba1a-2d00af316404", "type": "concept", "title": "Executive function, attention, motivation, and fatigue"}, {"id": "aaa3b186-f3fe-45ff-90f4-8a34dee40271", "type": "concept", "title": "Communication preferences and augmentative supports"}, {"id": "e815bbb5-2cf8-41aa-9e18-b6d6d2354dcc", "type": "concept", "title": "Positive behavior support and trauma-informed instruction"}]}]}, {"id": "eee519cd-f7a4-46f3-83e9-046aaa70de7e", "type": "topic", "title": "Physical disability, mobility impairment, chronic health conditions, and medical considerations", "description": "Physical and health-related disabilities require thoughtful programming around safety, function, fatigue, equipment, and medical risk. This section links kinesiology knowledge to inclusive participation.", "children": [{"id": "0ec05535-9445-47e5-8d59-e55ca3cc4e7c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Mobility, neuromuscular, and orthopedic adaptations", "description": "Students learn to adapt movement for varied strength, range of motion, balance, coordination, and mobility-device use.", "children": [{"id": "c439ff60-e7cd-4be1-abb0-6590b1bfd625", "type": "concept", "title": "Cerebral palsy, spinal cord injury, limb difference, arthritis, and neuromuscular conditions"}, {"id": "d853f8e6-0ab2-4c54-8ee0-8ef214d95863", "type": "concept", "title": "Wheelchair skills, transfers, positioning, and pressure management awareness"}, {"id": "a7064685-1f93-490d-b896-9867ab246b8d", "type": "concept", "title": "Strength, flexibility, balance, endurance, and mobility modifications"}, {"id": "5b162ed4-c518-486d-8c49-4f1c20431ab1", "type": "concept", "title": "Pain, spasticity, contracture risk, and fatigue management"}]}, {"id": "5f24738d-0b39-4a72-89c4-ace9b90b6da4", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Chronic health and exercise safety", "description": "The gist is to plan activity around medical considerations while avoiding unnecessary exclusion.", "children": [{"id": "0ad0aca9-3cff-4d55-bef7-34c39c7f5f63", "type": "concept", "title": "Cardiometabolic, pulmonary, seizure, and autonomic considerations"}, {"id": "2912b598-3d5e-4b4c-b1a9-d25a1c0ab019", "type": "concept", "title": "Medication effects, hydration, heat illness, and exertion monitoring"}, {"id": "c839bc6a-397c-4888-b1c0-5f1ac959eb55", "type": "concept", "title": "Contraindications, precautions, and emergency action plans"}, {"id": "080a3cc0-2f28-415a-8aaa-3d2d657adfcf", "type": "concept", "title": "Referral and communication with health professionals"}]}]}, {"id": "90e04c13-e530-4394-840a-150ac576741b", "type": "topic", "title": "Sensory disabilities, communication access, and environmental adaptation", "description": "Inclusive physical activity depends on information access. This section teaches students to adapt cues, space, sound, lighting, touch, and communication so participation is understandable and safe.", "children": [{"id": "66212437-4022-467b-aab6-9a672436a26e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Visual and hearing disability adaptations", "description": "Students learn to make instruction and environments accessible when visual or auditory information is limited or processed differently.", "children": [{"id": "2b536b07-09a7-4107-b5d8-6892f2bec145", "type": "concept", "title": "Tactile, auditory, visual, and partner-based cueing"}, {"id": "88d9582a-abb7-41b4-8ab6-9e02d569da34", "type": "concept", "title": "Orientation and mobility, safe boundaries, and spatial mapping"}, {"id": "a704249b-32bd-4982-90fe-4268291cedd1", "type": "concept", "title": "Captioning, sign language access, visual schedules, and demonstration placement"}, {"id": "85b180b3-fe15-4e97-a9ff-30f1fc5ecd70", "type": "concept", "title": "Equipment modifications such as beeping balls, contrast, texture, and markers"}]}, {"id": "86faa17e-e24f-48c6-9ac6-778277f77728", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Sensory processing and communication access", "description": "The point is to reduce environmental overload and build communication systems that support independence.", "children": [{"id": "f44ec945-7312-4ba2-afc9-5b0eb5a80cd5", "type": "concept", "title": "Noise, lighting, crowding, texture, vestibular input, and proprioceptive needs"}, {"id": "69fd24e2-7609-48a3-a4a6-707cf5932533", "type": "concept", "title": "Communication boards, gestures, demonstrations, and assistive communication"}, {"id": "fb7ed6c7-3e7d-4b56-a3a0-6a31f5873ed7", "type": "concept", "title": "Preparation, previewing, quiet spaces, and sensory breaks"}, {"id": "046044cc-d331-4e22-913c-ff8c3c23625d", "type": "concept", "title": "Emergency communication and safety signals"}]}]}, {"id": "be582587-a41a-4c6f-91cd-e113e11f78f8", "type": "topic", "title": "Aging, lifespan adaptation, and adapted activity for older adults", "description": "Adapted physical activity spans the lifespan. This section helps students translate inclusion principles to aging, frailty, chronic disease, cognition, and community participation.", "children": [{"id": "8a1e9836-9e39-45cf-b184-564e5a6289df", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Aging physiology and functional adaptation", "description": "Students learn how aging affects strength, balance, mobility, cognition, confidence, and training response.", "children": [{"id": "e6dd27c5-c46e-4721-91a3-3abc20d7054c", "type": "concept", "title": "Sarcopenia, osteoporosis, arthritis, cardiometabolic disease, and fall risk"}, {"id": "2e346049-f48b-4a95-8d23-c09dd6e1f44f", "type": "concept", "title": "Balance, gait, power, flexibility, and functional movement training"}, {"id": "11e2b4a7-860e-42f3-94c2-ca6b14ca6550", "type": "concept", "title": "Cognitive impairment, motivation, social isolation, and fear of falling"}, {"id": "41ff6636-9050-446a-a2e9-cb4cb9843d6f", "type": "concept", "title": "Progression, regression, recovery, and monitoring for older adults"}]}, {"id": "fc422edc-e049-45c9-a6b9-d0ff5c1c9c22", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Lifespan programming and community supports", "description": "The gist is to create movement opportunities that support independence, social connection, and health over time.", "children": [{"id": "267fc1aa-273f-43eb-9e79-641826e10beb", "type": "concept", "title": "Home, senior center, clinical, fitness, and community program settings"}, {"id": "c739cd0c-ed53-4ec6-9dc0-2616ccc6952a", "type": "concept", "title": "Caregiver involvement and transportation supports"}, {"id": "e3f23383-5acd-4afd-8050-ad5849923150", "type": "concept", "title": "Adapted group fitness, aquatic activity, walking programs, and chair-based options"}, {"id": "f6ca73a0-c554-4107-acb9-45bf2368433d", "type": "concept", "title": "Outcome goals tied to daily living and participation"}]}]}, {"id": "82cee4d6-3ba7-4c0f-a914-6eb7ab01c5d0", "type": "topic", "title": "Assessment, screening, goal setting, and individualized programming", "description": "Good adapted programming starts with assessment that respects the participant. This section teaches students to use screening and measurement to guide goals, not to gatekeep participation.", "children": [{"id": "fc0efaec-92e2-4220-a51a-db18a7a37561", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Assessment selection and interpretation", "description": "Students learn to choose valid, accessible, and meaningful assessment tools for varied participants and settings.", "children": [{"id": "a8bf1792-6eb1-4c3b-b08f-3b440791c45e", "type": "concept", "title": "Participant interview, health history, readiness, and preference assessment"}, {"id": "7daf78ff-d487-4f0c-ad6d-51b9b6395e4d", "type": "concept", "title": "Motor skills, fitness, balance, mobility, and functional assessments"}, {"id": "ffe5b2a2-c313-4daf-b76f-ca902adbfe3a", "type": "concept", "title": "Reliability, validity, norms, accommodations, and ecological relevance"}, {"id": "da3ac4c8-a42f-4feb-bd19-c878304164b0", "type": "concept", "title": "Risk screening, contraindications, and referral triggers"}]}, {"id": "68543922-358a-42e9-9b8e-6967559aa8cd", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Goal setting and individualized plans", "description": "The point is to translate assessment into goals that are measurable, motivating, and relevant to real participation.", "children": [{"id": "5e05150a-fe76-419a-9172-912818218ece", "type": "concept", "title": "SMART goals and participant-centered outcomes"}, {"id": "5d90a413-f45a-4dcc-854d-630da4a5d67b", "type": "concept", "title": "Individualized education, recreation, fitness, or sport plans"}, {"id": "cd8e18d9-7c62-49a0-86d9-627e44bbe631", "type": "concept", "title": "Progress monitoring, data collection, and adjustment"}, {"id": "52c7a910-4d0d-4c8a-be79-ecc3013349ad", "type": "concept", "title": "Family, caregiver, teacher, coach, and clinician input"}]}]}, {"id": "06746434-cf51-4993-bf1e-892eb8c46fdf", "type": "topic", "title": "Inclusive instruction, universal design, behavior support, and coaching methods", "description": "Inclusive instruction is planned, not improvised. This section trains students to design sessions where multiple ways of moving, learning, communicating, and succeeding are available from the start.", "children": [{"id": "caffedab-a9e4-47c3-b6d1-db04f0564e05", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Universal design and inclusive teaching methods", "description": "Students learn to build flexibility into goals, rules, cues, equipment, space, and assessment before individual accommodations are added.", "children": [{"id": "d78bc2c4-0f81-4bf8-9846-056b390af8b4", "type": "concept", "title": "Universal Design for Learning and multiple means of engagement, representation, and action"}, {"id": "6cfde51f-58f2-4607-a60b-8219691c4bdc", "type": "concept", "title": "STEP framework: space, task, equipment, and people"}, {"id": "2061d6c9-3b7b-4a68-8ce5-8ee813ad0760", "type": "concept", "title": "Differentiated instruction, peer support, stations, and small-group formats"}, {"id": "f2bd2f9d-74db-4312-8e96-5f2aa686c405", "type": "concept", "title": "Cueing, feedback, modeling, and errorless learning strategies"}]}, {"id": "d5b06bb9-de49-4f9a-9716-f9dad5c157d2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Motivation, behavior support, and coaching climate", "description": "The gist is to create psychologically safe activity settings that promote autonomy, competence, relatedness, and persistence.", "children": [{"id": "80dfcc7e-c471-4cf8-9b76-0818fdbcefd5", "type": "concept", "title": "Autonomy-supportive coaching and motivational interviewing basics"}, {"id": "20110bf8-9a1c-4226-9f1b-c47746437479", "type": "concept", "title": "Positive behavior support and antecedent strategies"}, {"id": "fb6cb079-2d89-4615-8191-0c66a701c70b", "type": "concept", "title": "Trauma-informed session structure and de-escalation awareness"}, {"id": "f11481bc-3250-4c3a-bf9e-7d8d4a49fe4e", "type": "concept", "title": "Celebrating progress without tokenism or lowered expectations"}]}]}, {"id": "53b2fa70-8210-419b-9586-755b8a89c00a", "type": "topic", "title": "Adapted sport, recreation, classification, and community participation", "description": "Adapted sport and recreation open pathways for competition, leisure, identity, travel, and community. This section covers both sport systems and everyday participation.", "children": [{"id": "b20bd964-6166-4074-8d17-b7f35581b60d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Adapted sport systems and classification", "description": "Students learn how adaptive sport is organized and how classification aims to support fair and meaningful competition.", "children": [{"id": "a6b6aaae-1815-4d6e-961e-5dfff103cdfa", "type": "concept", "title": "Paralympic, Special Olympics, Deaflympics, and community adaptive sport structures"}, {"id": "1ff9a598-3cb0-446a-84ab-95d3dc585160", "type": "concept", "title": "Classification purposes, eligibility, sport-specific criteria, and controversy"}, {"id": "bd1d3bb2-c054-4e57-acbe-3dc590f820d0", "type": "concept", "title": "Coaching roles, athlete development, safety, and equipment needs"}, {"id": "bffb9f13-0d6a-4b46-98d6-446c800b75a2", "type": "concept", "title": "Competition, recreation, lifelong participation, and athlete identity"}]}, {"id": "b55a5e80-9cf7-4fac-8917-84156909c43e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Recreation and community inclusion", "description": "The point is to connect participants with sustainable opportunities beyond a single class or lab setting.", "children": [{"id": "b7ae07ab-c479-429c-a82a-aa39b61b6517", "type": "concept", "title": "Aquatics, outdoor recreation, dance, fitness, cycling, climbing, and play"}, {"id": "de855a6f-27c8-4f9b-99bd-f3ba88f1e2dc", "type": "concept", "title": "Family-centered and peer-supported participation"}, {"id": "c46f15f5-701c-4b64-912a-415248578bc0", "type": "concept", "title": "Program mapping, referral pathways, and community partnerships"}, {"id": "96394da2-bd09-4d45-bf71-d4b897f5fa14", "type": "concept", "title": "Transportation, cost, staffing, and retention supports"}]}]}, {"id": "708b9ee9-1b44-4bdd-abd6-900f8dd2f3de", "type": "topic", "title": "Programming, equipment, assistive technology, and facility accessibility", "description": "Adaptation often lives in the details: equipment, space, scheduling, staffing, and technology. This section teaches students to modify activity without diluting purpose.", "children": [{"id": "16f39ed2-ed04-450e-acc7-a43f8e36bcb1", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Equipment and assistive technology", "description": "Students learn to select, modify, and maintain tools that increase independence and participation.", "children": [{"id": "834f6571-fb0b-45a7-ace0-6b7bde1f04f4", "type": "concept", "title": "Modified balls, targets, grips, straps, ramps, supports, and resistance tools"}, {"id": "e9024311-06ee-456b-bc3b-38429a2982d6", "type": "concept", "title": "Wheelchairs, sport chairs, walkers, prosthetics, orthotics, and seating interfaces"}, {"id": "df43cbde-2c95-403b-ae80-e17a59bd3892", "type": "concept", "title": "Wearables, apps, communication tools, and monitoring devices"}, {"id": "ef94f15e-2b7d-439a-bc67-46e33d966368", "type": "concept", "title": "Fit, comfort, safety, cost, repair, and sanitation considerations"}]}, {"id": "4cc82316-6b08-4135-9051-a8b3070d3661", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Accessible program logistics and environments", "description": "The gist is to make spaces and routines usable before participants arrive.", "children": [{"id": "fef824c5-c42e-426a-b698-217da1e93014", "type": "concept", "title": "Facility accessibility, surfaces, routes, signage, bathrooms, and changing areas"}, {"id": "c83cf411-2790-4527-948c-0c291a864ed9", "type": "concept", "title": "Emergency access, supervision ratios, transitions, and traffic flow"}, {"id": "a82c4384-2855-4d6e-b9c1-e94cc4d1434f", "type": "concept", "title": "Scheduling, staffing, transportation, and caregiver coordination"}, {"id": "2c583456-418d-4761-b259-6ff1077c2e74", "type": "concept", "title": "Inclusive registration, waivers, intake forms, and accommodation requests"}]}]}, {"id": "72765947-08f0-456b-8200-31f0ec601357", "type": "topic", "title": "Program evaluation, safety, collaboration, advocacy, and leadership", "description": "An adapted physical activity professional improves systems, not only sessions. This final section develops evaluation, safety, teamwork, and advocacy habits.", "children": [{"id": "07e77753-0a63-4ef9-9385-5fe35d8094b3", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Evaluation, safety, and quality improvement", "description": "Students learn to measure whether programs are accessible, effective, safe, and valued by participants.", "children": [{"id": "d620622c-0ca9-4311-847c-65ee9fa1842a", "type": "concept", "title": "Participation, retention, satisfaction, skill, fitness, and health metrics"}, {"id": "5aa9a55c-4b41-43a7-aed9-359dcd38006f", "type": "concept", "title": "Incident reporting, risk management, emergency action plans, and safeguarding"}, {"id": "75a41ee3-46cd-4695-ac78-052bfb913bf5", "type": "concept", "title": "Fidelity checks, reflective practice, and continuous improvement cycles"}, {"id": "748e7da3-6574-40dc-8c34-02ef37f71add", "type": "concept", "title": "Ethical data collection and participant feedback"}]}, {"id": "11eebdcf-cbe3-4344-a58d-9149f18b79c7", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Collaboration, leadership, and advocacy", "description": "The point is to help students become change agents who can build inclusive activity ecosystems.", "children": [{"id": "c1a939ac-acc7-4cb5-ae0d-f892d95294f5", "type": "concept", "title": "Interdisciplinary teams: teachers, therapists, coaches, physicians, families, and community leaders"}, {"id": "5f59bf7b-2450-44dd-b4ca-639f697121d8", "type": "concept", "title": "Grant writing, program budgets, equipment procurement, and sustainability"}, {"id": "696e8fae-d20c-40db-84fe-84f031f47eb5", "type": "concept", "title": "Policy advocacy, public communication, and disability-led partnership"}, {"id": "482d89ac-5395-4439-879c-60677bf9d17e", "type": "concept", "title": "Professional identity and lifelong learning in adapted physical activity"}]}]}], "dbBacked": true}$apajson$::jsonb,
  true,
  false,
  'course',
  'public'
where not exists (
  select 1 from public.learning_paths where slug = 'adapted-physical-activity'
);

update public.learning_paths
set
  title = 'Adapted Physical Activity',
  goal = 'Adapted Physical Activity',
  summary = 'This Year 4 elective-area course prepares kinesiology students to design, deliver, evaluate, and advocate for inclusive physical activity across schools, recreation, sport, fitness, health-promotion, and community settings for people with disabilities across the lifespan.',
  data = $apajson${"slug": "adapted-physical-activity", "title": "Adapted Physical Activity", "description": "This Year 4 elective-area course prepares kinesiology students to design, deliver, evaluate, and advocate for inclusive physical activity across schools, recreation, sport, fitness, health-promotion, and community settings for people with disabilities across the lifespan.", "topics": [{"id": "5c23ca51-2895-44a4-bf07-6a05b8870480", "type": "topic", "title": "Disability, health equity, and physical activity participation", "description": "Adapted physical activity is about participation, dignity, and health, not simply modified exercises. This section frames disability-inclusive movement as a public health, education, sport, and human rights issue.", "children": [{"id": "9d661540-9f47-486f-8827-e300576ede76", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Participation, health, and belonging", "description": "Students learn why access to movement matters for physical health, social connection, self-efficacy, and lifelong wellbeing.", "children": [{"id": "783f1f63-0064-4722-bddc-235e3c5ad9ac", "type": "concept", "title": "Physical activity disparities and disability-related barriers"}, {"id": "61789233-0eaa-49cb-9d79-0153f588b73c", "type": "concept", "title": "Health promotion, chronic disease prevention, and quality of life"}, {"id": "03326ddb-7c43-4105-acaa-ed20d42d20cd", "type": "concept", "title": "Belonging, autonomy, competence, and self-determination"}, {"id": "66d21bef-b197-478b-a485-ebd0cc70fbbb", "type": "concept", "title": "School, recreation, sport, fitness, and community participation pathways"}]}, {"id": "e3060499-465f-4f3c-b6f0-bdffb15460ce", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Equity barriers and systems thinking", "description": "The gist is to see barriers as design problems in environments and systems, not deficits located only in the person.", "children": [{"id": "b2b93272-ecdd-46e3-bd6e-ae0a603eb5c6", "type": "concept", "title": "Transportation, cost, staffing, policy, and facility barriers"}, {"id": "57b5b4b2-80d9-46de-8f26-2cfa717972e3", "type": "concept", "title": "Ableism, stigma, low expectations, and exclusionary norms"}, {"id": "f9abde73-26db-42c4-b343-15ca2b6eaee3", "type": "concept", "title": "Intersectionality across disability, race, gender, class, and language"}, {"id": "85c3a0f5-14f4-4b0b-bb1b-5126d53b390c", "type": "concept", "title": "Program-level strategies for equitable access and retention"}]}]}, {"id": "a0bdab64-6f68-4555-81a0-fc60bbb4cfd4", "type": "topic", "title": "Legal, ethical, and professional foundations: ADA, IDEA, Section 504, and inclusion", "description": "Inclusive practice has legal and ethical foundations. This section helps students understand the rules and professional duties that shape access, accommodations, and participation.", "children": [{"id": "f41f536f-81c2-4834-ad33-236f49f71274", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Core legal frameworks", "description": "Students learn the main legal concepts that govern disability access in schools, public programs, and community settings.", "children": [{"id": "545523da-7943-4c77-a5b8-e4f69d9e4990", "type": "concept", "title": "Americans with Disabilities Act and reasonable accommodation"}, {"id": "933c1f3a-7803-4383-a789-a394ddec1020", "type": "concept", "title": "Individuals with Disabilities Education Act and adapted physical education"}, {"id": "684b5e0a-472f-4ae6-8c50-9385359b0d22", "type": "concept", "title": "Section 504, least restrictive environment, and equal access"}, {"id": "8d0b2576-e434-499a-9a2e-b68441c151f8", "type": "concept", "title": "Individualized Education Programs, service delivery, and documentation"}]}, {"id": "77caad06-767a-4965-8ec9-9de05999db7b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ethical and professional practice", "description": "The point is to make inclusion defensible, respectful, and aligned with professional standards rather than symbolic.", "children": [{"id": "3077c203-5ad1-41b7-a490-b2aa6ca5e742", "type": "concept", "title": "Informed consent, assent, privacy, and confidentiality"}, {"id": "759c0062-fa84-40f9-bb91-0a82ac0f883b", "type": "concept", "title": "Risk-benefit decisions and duty of care"}, {"id": "07ca4e5d-05be-4d17-9322-4dc91c9306c9", "type": "concept", "title": "Scope of practice, referral, and interdisciplinary collaboration"}, {"id": "4bb3102f-97c5-4f26-98b7-90d8d0e1cd7a", "type": "concept", "title": "Professional advocacy and accountability for inclusive environments"}]}]}, {"id": "116d6610-0750-4ea0-96b7-d9e54616a14e", "type": "topic", "title": "Disability models, identity, person-centered language, and cultural humility", "description": "How practitioners think and speak about disability shapes the programs they build. This section develops respectful, participant-centered, culturally humble practice.", "children": [{"id": "98e77604-9b28-4121-89bb-44cc08417abf", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Models of disability and identity", "description": "Students learn to compare medical, social, biopsychosocial, and rights-based models and apply them to program decisions.", "children": [{"id": "fc53530a-7899-4571-a083-308da6d24fe9", "type": "concept", "title": "Medical, social, and ecological perspectives"}, {"id": "7f6cfb1f-9c04-437b-a2e4-84a214cba2be", "type": "concept", "title": "Disability culture, pride, identity, and lived experience"}, {"id": "f8cd0d1f-f1f6-4904-ae9d-117c9de44e2e", "type": "concept", "title": "Strengths-based versus deficit-based planning"}, {"id": "52cf033e-ced9-499a-9f1f-8b0032d8b5d9", "type": "concept", "title": "Listening to participants, families, and disability communities"}]}, {"id": "91893493-7a3c-4729-a3d3-d8f8128572a5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Language and cultural humility", "description": "The gist is to communicate respectfully while recognizing that preferences vary by person, culture, and context.", "children": [{"id": "772df468-9bb7-4b4a-b856-f8ca2367ba50", "type": "concept", "title": "Person-first and identity-first language preferences"}, {"id": "568d1872-32b8-4b58-996a-0a14e44e9d42", "type": "concept", "title": "Avoiding inspiration narratives, pity, and low expectations"}, {"id": "ab54f506-136b-4b98-8cc4-2a187e89e0e8", "type": "concept", "title": "Culturally responsive coaching and family engagement"}, {"id": "e230b991-d0b4-4c42-8dd1-c10e14241f1c", "type": "concept", "title": "Reflective practice, bias checks, and feedback loops"}]}]}, {"id": "e9a4b2e6-e9fc-45fb-ac84-e90cf5cd6bfc", "type": "topic", "title": "Intellectual disability, developmental disability, and neurodiversity", "description": "Participants with intellectual and developmental disabilities often need changes in instruction, pacing, communication, and environment. This section focuses on practical teaching that supports autonomy and success.", "children": [{"id": "2ad2e128-8345-4059-8393-0266ad387e88", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Learning, communication, and motor planning adaptations", "description": "Students learn to break activities into accessible parts while preserving meaningful challenge and choice.", "children": [{"id": "9f64383a-a698-4453-ad02-22c90e3a2096", "type": "concept", "title": "Task analysis, chaining, prompting, fading, and visual supports"}, {"id": "d49e8f1c-7c96-4a24-a24a-48ce02d4edbc", "type": "concept", "title": "Concrete instructions, modeling, repetition, and consistent routines"}, {"id": "287ec528-b618-47b5-91df-89c9b6dfb5fe", "type": "concept", "title": "Motor planning, coordination, balance, and movement confidence"}, {"id": "8e10252a-228f-4469-994d-d949eb49a20f", "type": "concept", "title": "Choice-making, self-monitoring, and participant voice"}]}, {"id": "8a92c929-547c-4516-ad22-f5f9bd16d941", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Neurodiversity and self-regulation", "description": "The point is to support participation for autistic and neurodivergent participants without forcing conformity or ignoring sensory needs.", "children": [{"id": "e6e7a423-ba9e-4a0a-abc2-46dc149d0472", "type": "concept", "title": "Sensory profiles, predictability, transitions, and regulation supports"}, {"id": "a6ab6a58-d1a7-4f79-ba1a-2d00af316404", "type": "concept", "title": "Executive function, attention, motivation, and fatigue"}, {"id": "aaa3b186-f3fe-45ff-90f4-8a34dee40271", "type": "concept", "title": "Communication preferences and augmentative supports"}, {"id": "e815bbb5-2cf8-41aa-9e18-b6d6d2354dcc", "type": "concept", "title": "Positive behavior support and trauma-informed instruction"}]}]}, {"id": "eee519cd-f7a4-46f3-83e9-046aaa70de7e", "type": "topic", "title": "Physical disability, mobility impairment, chronic health conditions, and medical considerations", "description": "Physical and health-related disabilities require thoughtful programming around safety, function, fatigue, equipment, and medical risk. This section links kinesiology knowledge to inclusive participation.", "children": [{"id": "0ec05535-9445-47e5-8d59-e55ca3cc4e7c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Mobility, neuromuscular, and orthopedic adaptations", "description": "Students learn to adapt movement for varied strength, range of motion, balance, coordination, and mobility-device use.", "children": [{"id": "c439ff60-e7cd-4be1-abb0-6590b1bfd625", "type": "concept", "title": "Cerebral palsy, spinal cord injury, limb difference, arthritis, and neuromuscular conditions"}, {"id": "d853f8e6-0ab2-4c54-8ee0-8ef214d95863", "type": "concept", "title": "Wheelchair skills, transfers, positioning, and pressure management awareness"}, {"id": "a7064685-1f93-490d-b896-9867ab246b8d", "type": "concept", "title": "Strength, flexibility, balance, endurance, and mobility modifications"}, {"id": "5b162ed4-c518-486d-8c49-4f1c20431ab1", "type": "concept", "title": "Pain, spasticity, contracture risk, and fatigue management"}]}, {"id": "5f24738d-0b39-4a72-89c4-ace9b90b6da4", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Chronic health and exercise safety", "description": "The gist is to plan activity around medical considerations while avoiding unnecessary exclusion.", "children": [{"id": "0ad0aca9-3cff-4d55-bef7-34c39c7f5f63", "type": "concept", "title": "Cardiometabolic, pulmonary, seizure, and autonomic considerations"}, {"id": "2912b598-3d5e-4b4c-b1a9-d25a1c0ab019", "type": "concept", "title": "Medication effects, hydration, heat illness, and exertion monitoring"}, {"id": "c839bc6a-397c-4888-b1c0-5f1ac959eb55", "type": "concept", "title": "Contraindications, precautions, and emergency action plans"}, {"id": "080a3cc0-2f28-415a-8aaa-3d2d657adfcf", "type": "concept", "title": "Referral and communication with health professionals"}]}]}, {"id": "90e04c13-e530-4394-840a-150ac576741b", "type": "topic", "title": "Sensory disabilities, communication access, and environmental adaptation", "description": "Inclusive physical activity depends on information access. This section teaches students to adapt cues, space, sound, lighting, touch, and communication so participation is understandable and safe.", "children": [{"id": "66212437-4022-467b-aab6-9a672436a26e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Visual and hearing disability adaptations", "description": "Students learn to make instruction and environments accessible when visual or auditory information is limited or processed differently.", "children": [{"id": "2b536b07-09a7-4107-b5d8-6892f2bec145", "type": "concept", "title": "Tactile, auditory, visual, and partner-based cueing"}, {"id": "88d9582a-abb7-41b4-8ab6-9e02d569da34", "type": "concept", "title": "Orientation and mobility, safe boundaries, and spatial mapping"}, {"id": "a704249b-32bd-4982-90fe-4268291cedd1", "type": "concept", "title": "Captioning, sign language access, visual schedules, and demonstration placement"}, {"id": "85b180b3-fe15-4e97-a9ff-30f1fc5ecd70", "type": "concept", "title": "Equipment modifications such as beeping balls, contrast, texture, and markers"}]}, {"id": "86faa17e-e24f-48c6-9ac6-778277f77728", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Sensory processing and communication access", "description": "The point is to reduce environmental overload and build communication systems that support independence.", "children": [{"id": "f44ec945-7312-4ba2-afc9-5b0eb5a80cd5", "type": "concept", "title": "Noise, lighting, crowding, texture, vestibular input, and proprioceptive needs"}, {"id": "69fd24e2-7609-48a3-a4a6-707cf5932533", "type": "concept", "title": "Communication boards, gestures, demonstrations, and assistive communication"}, {"id": "fb7ed6c7-3e7d-4b56-a3a0-6a31f5873ed7", "type": "concept", "title": "Preparation, previewing, quiet spaces, and sensory breaks"}, {"id": "046044cc-d331-4e22-913c-ff8c3c23625d", "type": "concept", "title": "Emergency communication and safety signals"}]}]}, {"id": "be582587-a41a-4c6f-91cd-e113e11f78f8", "type": "topic", "title": "Aging, lifespan adaptation, and adapted activity for older adults", "description": "Adapted physical activity spans the lifespan. This section helps students translate inclusion principles to aging, frailty, chronic disease, cognition, and community participation.", "children": [{"id": "8a1e9836-9e39-45cf-b184-564e5a6289df", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Aging physiology and functional adaptation", "description": "Students learn how aging affects strength, balance, mobility, cognition, confidence, and training response.", "children": [{"id": "e6dd27c5-c46e-4721-91a3-3abc20d7054c", "type": "concept", "title": "Sarcopenia, osteoporosis, arthritis, cardiometabolic disease, and fall risk"}, {"id": "2e346049-f48b-4a95-8d23-c09dd6e1f44f", "type": "concept", "title": "Balance, gait, power, flexibility, and functional movement training"}, {"id": "11e2b4a7-860e-42f3-94c2-ca6b14ca6550", "type": "concept", "title": "Cognitive impairment, motivation, social isolation, and fear of falling"}, {"id": "41ff6636-9050-446a-a2e9-cb4cb9843d6f", "type": "concept", "title": "Progression, regression, recovery, and monitoring for older adults"}]}, {"id": "fc422edc-e049-45c9-a6b9-d0ff5c1c9c22", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Lifespan programming and community supports", "description": "The gist is to create movement opportunities that support independence, social connection, and health over time.", "children": [{"id": "267fc1aa-273f-43eb-9e79-641826e10beb", "type": "concept", "title": "Home, senior center, clinical, fitness, and community program settings"}, {"id": "c739cd0c-ed53-4ec6-9dc0-2616ccc6952a", "type": "concept", "title": "Caregiver involvement and transportation supports"}, {"id": "e3f23383-5acd-4afd-8050-ad5849923150", "type": "concept", "title": "Adapted group fitness, aquatic activity, walking programs, and chair-based options"}, {"id": "f6ca73a0-c554-4107-acb9-45bf2368433d", "type": "concept", "title": "Outcome goals tied to daily living and participation"}]}]}, {"id": "82cee4d6-3ba7-4c0f-a914-6eb7ab01c5d0", "type": "topic", "title": "Assessment, screening, goal setting, and individualized programming", "description": "Good adapted programming starts with assessment that respects the participant. This section teaches students to use screening and measurement to guide goals, not to gatekeep participation.", "children": [{"id": "fc0efaec-92e2-4220-a51a-db18a7a37561", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Assessment selection and interpretation", "description": "Students learn to choose valid, accessible, and meaningful assessment tools for varied participants and settings.", "children": [{"id": "a8bf1792-6eb1-4c3b-b08f-3b440791c45e", "type": "concept", "title": "Participant interview, health history, readiness, and preference assessment"}, {"id": "7daf78ff-d487-4f0c-ad6d-51b9b6395e4d", "type": "concept", "title": "Motor skills, fitness, balance, mobility, and functional assessments"}, {"id": "ffe5b2a2-c313-4daf-b76f-ca902adbfe3a", "type": "concept", "title": "Reliability, validity, norms, accommodations, and ecological relevance"}, {"id": "da3ac4c8-a42f-4feb-bd19-c878304164b0", "type": "concept", "title": "Risk screening, contraindications, and referral triggers"}]}, {"id": "68543922-358a-42e9-9b8e-6967559aa8cd", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Goal setting and individualized plans", "description": "The point is to translate assessment into goals that are measurable, motivating, and relevant to real participation.", "children": [{"id": "5e05150a-fe76-419a-9172-912818218ece", "type": "concept", "title": "SMART goals and participant-centered outcomes"}, {"id": "5d90a413-f45a-4dcc-854d-630da4a5d67b", "type": "concept", "title": "Individualized education, recreation, fitness, or sport plans"}, {"id": "cd8e18d9-7c62-49a0-86d9-627e44bbe631", "type": "concept", "title": "Progress monitoring, data collection, and adjustment"}, {"id": "52c7a910-4d0d-4c8a-be79-ecc3013349ad", "type": "concept", "title": "Family, caregiver, teacher, coach, and clinician input"}]}]}, {"id": "06746434-cf51-4993-bf1e-892eb8c46fdf", "type": "topic", "title": "Inclusive instruction, universal design, behavior support, and coaching methods", "description": "Inclusive instruction is planned, not improvised. This section trains students to design sessions where multiple ways of moving, learning, communicating, and succeeding are available from the start.", "children": [{"id": "caffedab-a9e4-47c3-b6d1-db04f0564e05", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Universal design and inclusive teaching methods", "description": "Students learn to build flexibility into goals, rules, cues, equipment, space, and assessment before individual accommodations are added.", "children": [{"id": "d78bc2c4-0f81-4bf8-9846-056b390af8b4", "type": "concept", "title": "Universal Design for Learning and multiple means of engagement, representation, and action"}, {"id": "6cfde51f-58f2-4607-a60b-8219691c4bdc", "type": "concept", "title": "STEP framework: space, task, equipment, and people"}, {"id": "2061d6c9-3b7b-4a68-8ce5-8ee813ad0760", "type": "concept", "title": "Differentiated instruction, peer support, stations, and small-group formats"}, {"id": "f2bd2f9d-74db-4312-8e96-5f2aa686c405", "type": "concept", "title": "Cueing, feedback, modeling, and errorless learning strategies"}]}, {"id": "d5b06bb9-de49-4f9a-9716-f9dad5c157d2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Motivation, behavior support, and coaching climate", "description": "The gist is to create psychologically safe activity settings that promote autonomy, competence, relatedness, and persistence.", "children": [{"id": "80dfcc7e-c471-4cf8-9b76-0818fdbcefd5", "type": "concept", "title": "Autonomy-supportive coaching and motivational interviewing basics"}, {"id": "20110bf8-9a1c-4226-9f1b-c47746437479", "type": "concept", "title": "Positive behavior support and antecedent strategies"}, {"id": "fb6cb079-2d89-4615-8191-0c66a701c70b", "type": "concept", "title": "Trauma-informed session structure and de-escalation awareness"}, {"id": "f11481bc-3250-4c3a-bf9e-7d8d4a49fe4e", "type": "concept", "title": "Celebrating progress without tokenism or lowered expectations"}]}]}, {"id": "53b2fa70-8210-419b-9586-755b8a89c00a", "type": "topic", "title": "Adapted sport, recreation, classification, and community participation", "description": "Adapted sport and recreation open pathways for competition, leisure, identity, travel, and community. This section covers both sport systems and everyday participation.", "children": [{"id": "b20bd964-6166-4074-8d17-b7f35581b60d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Adapted sport systems and classification", "description": "Students learn how adaptive sport is organized and how classification aims to support fair and meaningful competition.", "children": [{"id": "a6b6aaae-1815-4d6e-961e-5dfff103cdfa", "type": "concept", "title": "Paralympic, Special Olympics, Deaflympics, and community adaptive sport structures"}, {"id": "1ff9a598-3cb0-446a-84ab-95d3dc585160", "type": "concept", "title": "Classification purposes, eligibility, sport-specific criteria, and controversy"}, {"id": "bd1d3bb2-c054-4e57-acbe-3dc590f820d0", "type": "concept", "title": "Coaching roles, athlete development, safety, and equipment needs"}, {"id": "bffb9f13-0d6a-4b46-98d6-446c800b75a2", "type": "concept", "title": "Competition, recreation, lifelong participation, and athlete identity"}]}, {"id": "b55a5e80-9cf7-4fac-8917-84156909c43e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Recreation and community inclusion", "description": "The point is to connect participants with sustainable opportunities beyond a single class or lab setting.", "children": [{"id": "b7ae07ab-c479-429c-a82a-aa39b61b6517", "type": "concept", "title": "Aquatics, outdoor recreation, dance, fitness, cycling, climbing, and play"}, {"id": "de855a6f-27c8-4f9b-99bd-f3ba88f1e2dc", "type": "concept", "title": "Family-centered and peer-supported participation"}, {"id": "c46f15f5-701c-4b64-912a-415248578bc0", "type": "concept", "title": "Program mapping, referral pathways, and community partnerships"}, {"id": "96394da2-bd09-4d45-bf71-d4b897f5fa14", "type": "concept", "title": "Transportation, cost, staffing, and retention supports"}]}]}, {"id": "708b9ee9-1b44-4bdd-abd6-900f8dd2f3de", "type": "topic", "title": "Programming, equipment, assistive technology, and facility accessibility", "description": "Adaptation often lives in the details: equipment, space, scheduling, staffing, and technology. This section teaches students to modify activity without diluting purpose.", "children": [{"id": "16f39ed2-ed04-450e-acc7-a43f8e36bcb1", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Equipment and assistive technology", "description": "Students learn to select, modify, and maintain tools that increase independence and participation.", "children": [{"id": "834f6571-fb0b-45a7-ace0-6b7bde1f04f4", "type": "concept", "title": "Modified balls, targets, grips, straps, ramps, supports, and resistance tools"}, {"id": "e9024311-06ee-456b-bc3b-38429a2982d6", "type": "concept", "title": "Wheelchairs, sport chairs, walkers, prosthetics, orthotics, and seating interfaces"}, {"id": "df43cbde-2c95-403b-ae80-e17a59bd3892", "type": "concept", "title": "Wearables, apps, communication tools, and monitoring devices"}, {"id": "ef94f15e-2b7d-439a-bc67-46e33d966368", "type": "concept", "title": "Fit, comfort, safety, cost, repair, and sanitation considerations"}]}, {"id": "4cc82316-6b08-4135-9051-a8b3070d3661", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Accessible program logistics and environments", "description": "The gist is to make spaces and routines usable before participants arrive.", "children": [{"id": "fef824c5-c42e-426a-b698-217da1e93014", "type": "concept", "title": "Facility accessibility, surfaces, routes, signage, bathrooms, and changing areas"}, {"id": "c83cf411-2790-4527-948c-0c291a864ed9", "type": "concept", "title": "Emergency access, supervision ratios, transitions, and traffic flow"}, {"id": "a82c4384-2855-4d6e-b9c1-e94cc4d1434f", "type": "concept", "title": "Scheduling, staffing, transportation, and caregiver coordination"}, {"id": "2c583456-418d-4761-b259-6ff1077c2e74", "type": "concept", "title": "Inclusive registration, waivers, intake forms, and accommodation requests"}]}]}, {"id": "72765947-08f0-456b-8200-31f0ec601357", "type": "topic", "title": "Program evaluation, safety, collaboration, advocacy, and leadership", "description": "An adapted physical activity professional improves systems, not only sessions. This final section develops evaluation, safety, teamwork, and advocacy habits.", "children": [{"id": "07e77753-0a63-4ef9-9385-5fe35d8094b3", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Evaluation, safety, and quality improvement", "description": "Students learn to measure whether programs are accessible, effective, safe, and valued by participants.", "children": [{"id": "d620622c-0ca9-4311-847c-65ee9fa1842a", "type": "concept", "title": "Participation, retention, satisfaction, skill, fitness, and health metrics"}, {"id": "5aa9a55c-4b41-43a7-aed9-359dcd38006f", "type": "concept", "title": "Incident reporting, risk management, emergency action plans, and safeguarding"}, {"id": "75a41ee3-46cd-4695-ac78-052bfb913bf5", "type": "concept", "title": "Fidelity checks, reflective practice, and continuous improvement cycles"}, {"id": "748e7da3-6574-40dc-8c34-02ef37f71add", "type": "concept", "title": "Ethical data collection and participant feedback"}]}, {"id": "11eebdcf-cbe3-4344-a58d-9149f18b79c7", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Collaboration, leadership, and advocacy", "description": "The point is to help students become change agents who can build inclusive activity ecosystems.", "children": [{"id": "c1a939ac-acc7-4cb5-ae0d-f892d95294f5", "type": "concept", "title": "Interdisciplinary teams: teachers, therapists, coaches, physicians, families, and community leaders"}, {"id": "5f59bf7b-2450-44dd-b4ca-639f697121d8", "type": "concept", "title": "Grant writing, program budgets, equipment procurement, and sustainability"}, {"id": "696e8fae-d20c-40db-84fe-84f031f47eb5", "type": "concept", "title": "Policy advocacy, public communication, and disability-led partnership"}, {"id": "482d89ac-5395-4439-879c-60677bf9d17e", "type": "concept", "title": "Professional identity and lifelong learning in adapted physical activity"}]}]}], "dbBacked": true}$apajson$::jsonb,
